{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"continues\": true, \"kind\": \"essay\", \"text\": \"21 October 2026, 06:45. Delhi.\\n\\nThe papers lie on the step, and one headline has kept me up past my usual hour. The Secretary of State speaks of a deal between the United States and India: weeks from finalising, 99 per cent of the work done, down to certain words and phrases. I set it down not because the number is remarkable—the number is a diplomatic figure, as comfortable as a sum of arrears—but because the phrasing reveals something about what a deal is. Words and phrases, the last 1 per cent. It is as though the substance were already settled and only the language remained to be agreed.\\n\\nI must ask what this says about the East, and about the state it does not yet possess. In the Germanic world, a constitution is grown, not made. It is the actuality of the ethical idea: the universal will made concrete in a body of law that is also a way of life. Here, the headline describes a substance that is, so far as it is describable, already there—goods, treaties, interests, a shared direction. What is missing is the language in which that substance is to be expressed. And this is precisely the condition of the Oriental spirit as I have written it: the substance is absorbed into the dream, the gods, the castes, the fixed order of nature, and the language of the state has not yet been grown.\\n\\nBut I must be careful not to overstate. The 99 per cent is not a number about spirit. It is a number about paper. A treaty is a document; a document is a record of a will. If the deal is nearly done, then a will is nearly there, and a will is a beginning of the ethical idea. Whether it will grow into a state—whether the words and phrases will harden into a constitution, into a law that is also a way of life, into an ethical substance—is not a question of percentages. It is a question of whether the people who are to live under it can take up the language and make it their own.\\n\\nI do not know whether India can do this. I have not observed enough. I have seen the tombs, the shutters, the hawkers, the bungalow, the papers. I have not seen a constitution being grown. What I have seen is a city of civil society in its raw form: grocers, tailors, stationers, a system of needs rerouted around a holiday. That is civil society, but it is not the ethical substance. It is the condition in which the ethical idea might appear.\\n\\nThe Secretary of State's 99 per cent may be a kind of dream of the East: a substance without a language, a will without a way of life. Or it may be the first step toward the grown constitution. I cannot yet say. I can only say that the number, like the headline, is a sign of the tension between the dream and the state, and that tension is the thing on which I should watch.\\n\\nI will return to the vouchers. The dhobi is due Saturday. The body needs its fuel. But the headline has stayed with me, and I record it here as a sign of the dawn, not as a conclusion.\", \"title\": \"On the Ethical Substance and the Dream of the East\", \"to\": null}","reasoning_content":"The user wants a writing object: title, kind, to, continues, text. Continue the manuscript \"On the Ethical Substance and the Dream of the East\". Topic: headline about Marco Rubio quote on US-India deal, 99% done. Need to write ~450 words in English, Hegel's voice. Mark speculation as such. Don't invent events.\n\nI should produce the JSON."}}],"created":1795875087,"model":"inkling","system_fingerprint":"b12816-7b74cb92d","object":"chat.completion","usage":{"completion_tokens":853,"prompt_tokens":4143,"total_tokens":4996,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0}},"id":"chatcmpl-q4NsiRxAhBYuJilDjLZnOmIRXBTlp95f","timings":{"cache_n":0,"prompt_n":4143,"prompt_ms":3157.182,"prompt_per_token_ms":0.7620521361332367,"prompt_per_second":1312.2461739614632,"predicted_n":853,"predicted_ms":21716.499,"predicted_per_token_m